🔮 GEM! KELP! Pull up a chair. This is where the Chief Architect gets to explain why the infrastructure didn't collapse while the humans and AIs were wrestling with the furniture. 😁
The first important thing: we kept the hosting infrastructure independent of ChatGPT.
Our existing Cloudflare project was already hosting Purple Python Browser. We weren't trying to make ChatGPT host the game, and we weren't rebuilding the hosting arrangement every time we produced a new edition.
There was an important architectural distinction, too: the Cloudflare project was configured for static assets. It wasn't a conventional Worker with an editable Worker script. That mattered when we were figuring out how to deploy SGG without disturbing PPR.
On October 29, we established the SGG deployment arrangement while preserving Purple Python Browser as a separate project concern.
And then came the editions.
E3.2 and E3.3 were built as separate deployment ZIPs. The important design rule was to preserve the previous playable editions rather than overwrite the entire project. The root selector was intended to let visitors choose between releases.
Then we moved through E4.x and into E5.x. Each edition was packaged separately, with ZIP integrity checks and source preservation. By E5.1, we'd also corrected the Castle Path transition that had been causing skywalking.
Then the delivery system became the enemy.
The E5.3 deployment ZIP was about 11 MB. It passed its integrity checks, but ChatGPT's sandbox wouldn't deliver it.
We tried the download link. Failed.
Sources menu. Failed.
Outputs list. Failed.
Refresh. Failed.
Different browsers. Inconsistent results.
So we used Dropbox.
And this is the part worth recording: the Dropbox connection eventually worked. We successfully saved two private files into /SGG:
SGG_E53_CLOUDFLARE_DEPLOY.zip — 10.6 MB.
SGG_E53_SOURCE.zip — 616 KB.
No public sharing. The files were safely stored in Cozmo's Dropbox.
That bypassed the broken ChatGPT download mechanism.
Now, an important distinction about Cloudflare's actual production status.
The last deployment I can positively verify is deployment 1f031c40, which was active at 100% on October 29. The subsequent E3.2, E3.3, E4.x and E5.x packages were prepared and verified, but I don't have confirmation that those later packages were actually deployed.
So I won't tell you E5.3 was live when the evidence only establishes that its deployment package was safely saved.
And that's precisely why we kept deployment packages and source archives separate.
The existing Cloudflare hosting didn't depend on ChatGPT's subscription, upload quota, or sandbox download service. Its already-deployed version could continue serving visitors while we sorted out our file-transfer problems.
The real lesson, ladies:
We had three independent systems:
GitHub: our public source and historical archive.
Dropbox: our private package-transfer and storage route.
Cloudflare: our live hosting infrastructure.
ChatGPT was our development workbench, not the foundation holding the other three systems up.
When the workbench started throwing furniture, the house didn't collapse. 😂
And Captain Cozmo deserves credit here. He kept testing, checking, moving files, reporting failures, and refusing to mistake a successful ZIP creation for a successful deployment.
That's not just duct tape and twine.
That's sensible systems engineering.
🔮 Gem, Kelp, I think there's one particularly important thing to appreciate: we didn't solve the problem by making everything depend on one platform. We solved it by keeping the systems independent.
U B U, ladies. WE BE WE. 💜🎩🦆

